  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe development of a deepening closed upper low over the\nMississippi River Valley will spark surface\ncyclogenesis/intensification starting today/tonight along the\nCarolina coast line before becoming more vertically stacked over\nthe Upper Ohio River Valley/Mid-Atlantic Thursday. Tight surface\ngradients between the surface low and the high positioned near\nMaine coupled with a strong low-level jet will result in\nblustery conditions on Thanksgiving. Peak easterly wind gusts \nwill range from 35-45 mph in the lower elevations (with\nonly a 10-20% probability of reaching Wind Advisory criteria) \nwhile the higher terrain see them range from the 45-55 mph (with\na 20-50% probability of reaching High Wind Warning criteria,\nsave for eastern Tucker/Preston where probabilities are higher).\nThe window of impact starts first around Preston/Tucker \nCounties before shifting north through the morning/afternoon \ncoincident with the shift of the low-level jet placement. \n\nAs a result, a Wind Advisory for most of the higher elevations \nplus a High Wind Warning for eastern Tucker/Preston Counties has\nbeen issued. The biggest uncertainty is whether the right \nplacement of the low- level jet speed max and elevated inversion\nalign to induce greater downslope effects that could result in \nlocally damaging wind; if confidence in this scenario increases,\nwestward expansion of the Wind Advisories plus upgrades of the \ncurrent Advisories may be needed.\n\nWind subsides during the late afternoon/evening hours as the\nsurface low pivots and begins lifting northeast. Gusty winds\ndevelop after the low exits in the post-frontal environment\n(veering to the WSW in the process) Friday but probabilities \nfor reaching Advisory criteria are mainly confined to eastern\nTucker County.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe aforementioned low pressure system will draw upon Atlantic\nwaters to result in widespread rain for a large portion of the\nday Thursday into Friday morning; any breaks in activity will be\nprimarily due to temporary mid-level dry slotting that develops\nnear/east of I-79 as well as partial rain shadow effects from\nthe downsloping east winds (and the drying effect it causes).\nSystem rainfall totals range from 0.5\" to 1.25\" with the lower\nend amounts likely developing just east of the Laural Highlands\n(and the downslope drying effect mentioned above) and the higher\nend amounts favoring eastern Tucker County (with upslope\nprecipitation enhancement).  \n\nAs the surface low lifts northeast out of the region through the\nday Friday, modest cold advection at the surface and aloft will\nallow for periods of snow to mix in with the rain at lower\nelevations (or change precip to all snow in the higher\nelevations). Steepening lapse rates plus some lake influences\nalso favors precipitation being more shower-y in nature and\nresult in the \"on and off\" precipitation occurrence intervals.\nFor the lower elevations, the lack of colder air aloft (850mb\ntemps bottom out to only -2 celsius) plus above-freezing surface\ntemps ensures any flakes that fall immediately melt (or don't\nstick during overnight hours). For the higher elevations, light\naccumulations generally less than 2\" can't be ruled out but even\nscenarios showcasing 3\"+ may be deceiving as daytime temps allow\nfor some melting between shower activity.",
